steps.body:
Inheritance builds a new class on top of an existing one. `class Dog : public Animal` makes Dog a kind of Animal: every Dog contains all of Animal's members, outside code can call Animal's public methods on it, and Dog can add its own.

```cpp
class Dog : public Animal {
public:
    Dog(const std::string& name, const std::string& toy)
        : Animal(name), toy_(toy) {} // base constructor first
    void fetch() const;              // new member
private:
    std::string toy_;
};
```

- Write `public`. With `class`, leaving it out means private inheritance, and outside code can no longer call Animal's methods on a Dog.
- `private` members of the base still exist inside every Dog, but Dog's own methods cannot touch them. Mark them `protected` when derived classes need them.
- Call the base constructor in the initializer list, as `Animal(name)`. If you leave it out, the compiler calls Animal's default constructor, which Animal does not have, so it will not compile. The base part is always built first, then the derived members, then the derived constructor body runs.

In memory, a Dog is one object: with g++ the Animal part comes first, followed by `toy_`. This is also the `class MyError : public std::runtime_error` recipe from Exceptions: MyError inherits `what()` and passes the message to the base constructor.

example.code:
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

class Animal {
public:
    explicit Animal(const std::string& name) : name_(name) {
        std::cout << "Animal " << name_ << " created\n";
    }
    void describe() const {
        std::cout << name_ << " is an animal\n";
    }

protected:
    std::string name_;
};

class Dog : public Animal {
public:
    Dog(const std::string& name, const std::string& toy) : Animal(name), toy_(toy) {
        std::cout << "Dog " << name_ << " created\n";
    }
    void fetch() const {
        std::cout << name_ << " fetches the " << toy_ << '\n';
    }

private:
    std::string toy_;
};

int main() {
    const Dog rex("Rex", "ball");
    rex.describe();
    rex.fetch();
    return 0;
}

